To use ashmem file descriptor in C++, you can use the standard file descriptor functions, such as open(), close(), read(), and write().
You can also use mmap() function to map the ashmem file descriptor to a memory address, then you can read and write to the shared memory as if it were a regular memory block.

Here is an example of how to use ashmem file descriptor in C++:
```
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>

int ashmem_fd = open("/dev/ashmem", O_RDWR);
if (ashmem_fd < 0) {
    // handle error
}

size_t size = 1024;
ioctl(ashmem_fd, ASHMEM_SET_SIZE, size);

void* addr = mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, ashmem_fd, 0);
if (addr == MAP_FAILED) {
    // handle error
}

// write data to the shared memory
sprintf((char*) addr, "Hello Ashmem!");

// read data from the shared memory
printf("Data: %s\n", (char*) addr);

// unmap the shared memory
munmap(addr, size);

// close the ashmem file descriptor
